window.MOCK_INI="; d3d12async settings. Put next to d3d12.dll in the game's executable folder.\n[d3d12async]\n; 0 = pure pass-through (the DLL loads but changes nothing)\nEnabled=1\n\n; How long a PSO creation may block before the game gets a placeholder instead.\n; Driver-cache hits finish in well under 1 ms, so they never get placeholders.\nBudgetMs=1\n\n; Which threads get placeholders:\n;   all       - every thread (needed for Unreal Engine 5, which creates runtime PSOs on task threads)\n;   recording - only threads that record command lists (closest to stock behaviour)\nAsyncThreads=all\n\n; Once this many compiles are waiting, non-recording threads block as usual, so a precache\n; flood can't pile up memory. Recording threads are never held back.\nMaxQueuedCompiles=256\n\n; What happens once MaxQueuedCompiles compiles are waiting (toggle live in game: Ctrl+Shift+P):\n;   compile     - the create compiles on the game's thread, as without the DLL (can hitch).\n;   placeholder - it still gets a placeholder, but only while fewer than MaxPlaceholders\n;                 placeholders are outstanding (not compiled yet); past that it compiles normally.   [default]\n;                 Gameplay bursts stay below the limit (Borderlands 4 peaked at ~430), while a\n;                 loading screen or precache flood (thousands) mostly compiles normally, so the\n;                 screen still waits for its shaders.\nQueueFullMode=placeholder\nMaxPlaceholders=512\n\n; EXPERIMENTAL - 1 = compile threads run in Windows 11 efficiency mode (EcoQoS: efficiency cores /\n; lower clocks) with low memory priority, leaving more CPU to the game. Compiles finish later.\n; Default 1 (the best Borderlands 4 run: 0 shader hitches in 13 minutes of gameplay).\nWorkerEfficiencyMode=1\n\n; EXPERIMENTAL - start at most this many background compiles per second (0 = no limit). Spreads\n; compile work out to leave CPU headroom; compiles a draw is waiting for are never held back.\n; Pair it with QueueFullMode=placeholder, otherwise the queue fills sooner and more creates\n; compile on the game's thread.\nMaxCompilesPerSecond=0\n\n; Compute pipelines (culling, particles, Lumen, Nanite...) can't be skipped:\n;   sync - compiled on the calling thread, like the game does on its own (many in parallel).\n;   wait - compiled in the background; a dispatch that needs one waits for it. Only helps games\n;          that precache compute well ahead of use. In Townfall it made level loads far worse:\n;          the render thread ended up compiling hundreds of them one by one.\n;   skip - EXPERIMENTAL: compiled in the background, and dispatches that use one are skipped until\n;          it's ready (like draws). No waiting at all, but the skipped work is missing: expect\n;          flicker, missing particles/effects or wrong lighting for a few frames, possibly worse.   [default]\nComputePipelineMode=skip\n\n; Startup precompile: while the game is compiling its shaders at launch, every PSO compiles\n; normally (stock behaviour, nothing skipped). The DLL detects when that flood is over - at least\n; StartupMinPsos created, then StartupQuietSeconds seconds in a row with no more than\n; StartupQuietRate new PSOs each - and only then starts compiling in the background. No restart.\nStartupCompileNormally=1\nStartupMinPsos=64\nStartupQuietSeconds=10\nStartupQuietRate=10\n; A second also counts as busy while the game's CPU use (percent of all CPUs) is above this:\n; a precompile keeps the CPU near 100%, so the phase can't end while the game is still compiling\n; through routes the DLL can't see. 0 = ignore CPU.\nStartupCpuBusyPercent=80\n; The phase can't end before the precompile itself has been seen: at least StartupFloodSeconds busy\n; seconds after the game's first frame (quiet intro videos before the compile screen don't count).\n; If no precompile starts within StartupFloodWaitSeconds of the first frame (caches already warm),\n; the normal quiet rule applies anyway. 0 = don't wait for a precompile.\nStartupFloodSeconds=10\nStartupFloodWaitSeconds=120\n; For this many seconds after launch, nothing but the Ctrl+Shift+End hotkey can end the startup\n; phase; after that, normal detection carries on if the game is still precompiling. Borderlands 4\n; can go quiet for ~17 s between its intro and its compile screen. 0 = off.\nStartupMinSeconds=150\n; Ctrl+Shift+End ends the startup phase by hand (e.g. if a game keeps compiling into gameplay).\nStartupHotkey=1\n; Optional hard limit on the startup phase in seconds (0 = none). Only a safety net for games\n; whose CPU use never drops.\nStartupMaxSeconds=0\n; During the startup phase, the game thread that is compiling a pipeline runs at the highest\n; thread priority for the length of that compile, so compiling gets the CPU before the rest of the game.\nStartupBoostCompileThreads=1\n; Frame-rate cap while the startup precompile runs (0 = off). Fewer frames to render leaves more\n; CPU for compiling. Lifted automatically the moment the precompile is over; never applies in gameplay.\nStartupFpsCap=30\n\n; EXPERIMENTAL, off by default (game-dependent). Loading screens: while the game shows no new frame for LoadingGapMs, pipelines compile normally\n; (stock behaviour), so a loading screen waits for its shaders instead of finishing early and\n; leaving them to compile in gameplay. After a gap of at least 2x LoadingGapMs (a real loading\n; screen rather than a hitch), this continues for LoadingHoldMs after frames resume, while the\n; level fades in. Placeholders are only used while the game is actually presenting frames.\n; Loading-screen frames are left out of the gameplay stats in the run logs.\nLoadingCompileNormally=0\nLoadingGapMs=500\nLoadingHoldMs=1500\n\n; UI-type pipelines (only 8-bit colour targets, no depth: menus, text, HUD, cached widget images):\n;   wait  - compiled in the background like the rest, but a draw that needs one waits for it\n;           (compiling it on the drawing thread if no worker has started it). Never skipped, and a\n;           burst of new UI pipelines compiles in parallel instead of one by one.   [default]\n;   sync  - compiled on the calling thread, one by one (older behaviour; can hitch on menus)\n;   async - treated like any other pipeline: draws are skipped until ready (UI can stay empty)\nUiPipelineMode=wait\n\n; With UiPipelineMode=wait: a draw waits at most this many ms for its UI pipeline, then is skipped\n; like any other draw (0 = wait for the whole compile). Avoids ~100 ms freezes when a menu or HUD\n; screen opens for the first time; the price is a UI element that can stay empty until the game\n; redraws it (Borderlands 4 once had an empty settings menu from a skipped UI draw).\nUiWaitMaxMs=2\n\n; Background compile threads. 0 = auto (a quarter of the CPU's hardware threads, 2..8).\nWorkers=2\n; Extra compile threads that only work while more than BurstQueue compiles are waiting (a level\n; load or precache wave), then go idle again. auto = as many as WorkerCpus allows (max 8); 0 = none.\nBurstWorkers=auto\nBurstQueue=16\nLowPriorityWorkers=1\n\n; Which logical CPUs the compile threads may run on (like Task Manager's \"Set affinity\"):\n;   upper-half  - the last half (on a 16-thread CPU: CPU 8-15)   [default]\n;   all         - any CPU\n;   8-15  or  8,10,12-15  - an explicit list\n; Only the compile threads are restricted; the game's own threads are untouched.\nWorkerCpus=upper-half\n\n; Small label on the left side of the game window showing the layer is loaded.\n; Visible in windowed/borderless mode (not exclusive fullscreen). Click-through; never touches the game's frames.\nOverlay=1\nOverlayStats=1\nOverlayOnlyWhenFocused=0\nOverlayOpacity=200\n; Frame-time graph in the label: green = normal, amber = hitch, red = hitch caused by a pipeline compile.\nOverlayGraph=1\n; Small settings panel in the bottom left of the game window. Settings are changed with hotkeys\n; while the game has focus (the overlay is click-through): Ctrl+Shift+P = queue-full placeholders on/off.\n; The hotkey works with the panel hidden too (the switch is written to d3d12async.log).\nSettingsPanel=0\n; Gameplay frames at least this slow are recorded as hitches (label, d3d12async.log, *_hitches.csv).\nHitchMs=50\n\n; Run logs, in d3d12async-runs\\ next to the DLL: one .csv (per second: fps, frame times, PSOs,\n; CPU) and one .txt summary per launch, labelled dll-ON or dll-OFF. runs.txt lists all kept runs,\n; newest first, marked LATEST. A \"dll-OFF\" run is a launch with Enabled=0: the DLL then changes\n; nothing and only measures (MeasureWhenDisabled=1), which gives a fair baseline.\nRunLogs=1\nRunLogsKeepOn=1\nRunLogsKeepOff=1\nMeasureWhenDisabled=1\n\n; error | warn | info | perf | debug   (perf logs every placeholder; use for diagnosis only)\nLogLevel=info\nSlowCompileLogMs=50\n\n; Testing only: delay every background compile by this many ms, to see what skipped draws look like.\nDebugCompileDelayMs=0\n";
